btc-miner for Debian
====================

What this is
------------

contrib/signet/miner from the Bitcoin Core source tree, packaged so a
signet can be mined from an installed system rather than a source
checkout. The bitcoin-core package has never shipped it, and there is no
RPC that replaces it: signet blocks carry a signature made with the
chain's challenge key, and Core has no call that produces one.

It does no hashing. It drives the node from outside:

  getblocktemplate    the candidate block
  walletprocesspsbt   the challenge signature
  bitcoin-util grind  the proof-of-work
  submitblock         publication

The challenge key therefore lives in a bitcoind wallet on the mining
host. The host that mines is the host holding the key that defines the
chain.

The local patch
---------------

The script is upstream's, MIT licensed, Copyright (c) 2020-present The
Bitcoin Core developers, with one local change:

  signet: let the miner target the chain's block interval
  Rene Malmgren <duke_h3@dwdc.club>

Upstream hardcodes the target spacing:

  INTERVAL = 600.0*2016/2015

The patch replaces that with a --block-interval argument defaulting to
the same 600 seconds, so behaviour is unchanged unless the argument is
given. It is the miner-side counterpart to the -signetblocktime patch
carried by bitcoin-core; see that package's README.Debian.

The two must agree
------------------

A chain configured with

  signetblocktime=60

must be mined with

  btc-miner ... generate --block-interval=60

Nothing enforces this. The miner paces itself from --block-interval and
the node's difficulty adjustment expects blocks to arrive at the rate
-signetblocktime names. If they disagree the chain still mines; it just
drifts away from its own target, retargeting every 2016 blocks toward a
spacing nobody asked for.

Layout
------

The script is installed unmodified, under a directory that preserves the
source tree's relative arrangement:

  /usr/share/btc-miner/contrib/signet/miner
  /usr/share/btc-miner/test/functional/test_framework/

It locates its imports with realpath, so /usr/bin/btc-miner is a symlink
into that tree and the script needs no patching to find its framework.
Nothing here is a modified copy of anything.

Version pin
-----------

This package depends on exactly the bitcoin-core version it was built
from, because the script imports that version's own functional test
framework. A miner from one version driving a node from another is not a
supported combination.
